278
00:10:42,150 --> 00:10:42,510
Thank you.

279
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:44,400
So let's get started.

280
00:10:45,170 --> 00:10:47,090
Tell me a bit about your educational background.

281
00:10:47,870 --> 00:10:48,350
Sure.

282
00:10:48,470 --> 00:10:55,830
Well, I graduated with honors from Chesterson University with a major in business administration with a specialization in information management.

283
00:10:56,430 --> 00:10:57,510
And I minored in psychology.

284
00:10:58,170 --> 00:10:59,910
I chose this course of study for two reasons.

285
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:04,510
I wanted to gain some practical, marketable skills, which the information management track provided.

286
00:11:05,330 --> 00:11:10,290
And I also feel that the interpersonal skills are essential for professional success, hence the minor in psychology.

287
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:11,460
Interesting.

288
00:11:12,180 --> 00:11:13,600
And your postgraduate studies?

289
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:16,980
Well, I am really passionate about consumer behavior.

290
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:19,160
So I pursued a master's in that area.

291
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:21,880
I also strive to keep my professional skills current.

292
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:26,660
So I continuously attend seminars and conferences related to management and customer service.

293
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:30,320
Very good.

294
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:34,860
Now, tell me a little bit about your work experience.

295
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:37,840
I see here that you previously worked at Oracle.

296
00:11:38,730 --> 00:11:39,230
Yeah.

297
00:11:39,230 --> 00:11:44,890
I worked as their customer support manager, which brought me a breadth of experience in both client care and process management.

298
00:11:45,430 --> 00:11:51,850
I supervised and coordinated the customer support team, as well as implemented new strategies to achieve better customer satisfaction.

299
00:11:52,890 --> 00:11:53,450
Interesting.

300
00:11:54,670 --> 00:11:55,030
Yes.

301
00:11:55,030 --> 00:11:59,910
In this position, I was able to make some pretty significant contributions to the overall success of the company.

302
00:12:00,370 --> 00:12:07,130
With the different initiatives that we implemented, we lowered our churn rate to about 5%, which had a direct impact on revenue.

303
00:12:14,830 --> 00:12:15,270
All right.

304
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:17,210
Well, I want to talk about two main things.

305
00:12:17,720 --> 00:12:22,950
And the first thing I want to talk about is how you address this question about your educational background.

306
00:12:23,640 --> 00:12:26,280
Like, what is the interviewer looking to find out?

307
00:12:26,280 --> 00:12:27,070
Okay, perfect.

308
00:12:27,430 --> 00:12:33,910
So when you are talking about your educational background, you shouldn't only talk about your college degree, right?

309
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:35,200
Yeah.

310
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:38,440
You should talk about any type of training that you've had.

311
00:12:38,490 --> 00:12:38,850
Yeah.

312
00:12:38,850 --> 00:12:46,670
I think it's really important to let the interviewer know that you're continually trying to update your skills and improve your knowledge.

313
00:12:47,130 --> 00:12:52,450
So any sort of courses or seminars that you might attend, this is a great chance to highlight those.

314
00:12:52,810 --> 00:12:54,550
And that's what the interviewer is looking for, right?

315
00:12:55,070 --> 00:13:03,530
Also, any trade publications, any magazines that you read related to your job is a great way to sort of highlight your desire to keep learning.

316
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:24,070
Well, I think that's really important because it's good that you went to a good university, you got this degree and everything, but continuously training yourself and maybe just by listening to business podcasts or reading specific magazines or stuff like that, that is demonstrating that you care about what you do and that you are continuously learning, right?

317
00:13:24,250 --> 00:13:24,430
Exactly.

318
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:26,680
Okay.

319
00:13:26,680 --> 00:13:30,280
So those are the main things to talk about in your educational background.

320
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:34,490
Now when this work experience question pops up, what should you really say?

321
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:39,090
When you're answering this question, I think there's something really important to keep in mind.

322
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:40,230
What's that?

323
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:44,170
You want to make sure that you're being as specific as possible, right?

324
00:13:44,640 --> 00:13:45,640
Right.

325
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:48,280
So giving examples of what you've done.

326
00:13:48,280 --> 00:13:54,050
Like really specifically what you did, all of the sort of the main achievements you have.

327
00:13:54,630 --> 00:13:55,030
Okay.

328
00:13:55,050 --> 00:14:05,080
So like for example, we saw in our dialogue that he talked about specific strategies that he implemented to improve customer support in that company.

329
00:14:05,280 --> 00:14:05,440
Right.

330
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:06,740
And then he gave some numbers.

331
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:08,360
Right.

332
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:11,580
He said, yeah, he lowered the churn rate to about 5%.

333
00:14:11,580 --> 00:14:22,750
So it's so important whenever you can attach a number to your success or your achievement to be able to do that in the interview really, really demonstrates your achievement.

334
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:24,320
Yeah.

335
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:25,390
I think that's really important.

336
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:33,040
And another thing when you're talking about previous jobs is make sure you keep it on a positive note.

337
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:41,710
Even if your experience was not very good or you didn't like the company or you had problems, you should avoid talking about that and always keep it on a positive note.

338
00:14:41,990 --> 00:14:42,130
Right.

339
00:14:42,430 --> 00:14:51,410
So if the interviewer asks you why you want to join this company, why you're looking to leave your current job, you want to give these positive reasons.

340
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:52,520
Right.

341
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:57,680
I didn't do well because my boss was a jerk or I didn't like the company anymore.

342
00:14:57,680 --> 00:15:06,920
You know, you should answer it with something positive like, well, I think I need a change or this job opportunity is more aligned with what I want to do in the future, etc.

343
00:15:06,920 --> 00:15:07,920
Yeah.

344
00:15:07,920 --> 00:15:13,310
So positive always on your interviews and just again, prepare for these questions that are always going to be there.

345
00:15:13,720 --> 00:15:14,720
Yes.

346
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:21,040
So Erica, I know that you've done a lot of interviews and I understand that you've had a really weird question.

347
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:21,370
Yes.

348
00:15:21,730 --> 00:15:23,990
In an interview, I once got this question.

349
00:15:25,240 --> 00:15:28,250
If you were a tree, what kind of tree would you be?

350
00:15:28,810 --> 00:15:29,170
Really?

351
00:15:29,470 --> 00:15:30,050
Yeah, I'm not kidding.

352
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:31,680
I really was asked that question.

353
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:32,150
What did you answer?

354
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:36,850
I think I said that I would want to be a willow tree because they're really beautiful.

355
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:39,030
Was that the right answer?

356
00:15:39,370 --> 00:15:39,990
I don't know.

357
00:15:40,240 --> 00:15:41,970
Like, what are you supposed to answer to that question?

358
00:15:42,240 --> 00:15:44,040
I really don't know what the right answer was.

359
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:49,480
Well, some people, yeah, they'll ask you just, I don't know, random questions just to see what your response is.

360
00:15:49,880 --> 00:15:54,380
I think also maybe it's just how you deal with the pressure.

361
00:15:54,900 --> 00:15:56,140
With the unexpected questions.

362
00:15:56,520 --> 00:15:57,520
Right.

363
00:15:57,520 --> 00:16:00,920
So it's like, that is pretty unexpected, but I guess you handle it pretty well.

364
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:01,140
Okay.

365
00:16:01,920 --> 00:16:02,920
Well, I mean, I got the job, so.

366
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:03,120
Oh, nice.

367
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:04,920
All right.

368
00:16:04,920 --> 00:16:05,920
All right.

369
00:16:05,920 --> 00:16:09,880
I want to know from our listeners, if they were a tree, what kind of tree they would be?

370
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:11,160
Yeah.

371
00:16:11,160 --> 00:16:15,380
And also tell us about, well, your job experience or your educational background.

372
00:16:16,460 --> 00:16:19,500
Well, I know for example, that I've had a lot of different types of jobs.

373
00:16:19,720 --> 00:16:23,260
I've even been that person that gets on the bus and charges people the money.

374
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:23,820
I know.

375
00:16:24,520 --> 00:16:29,200
Like, you've had every job under the sun.

376
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:30,160
So, but it's great.

377
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:33,020
So tell us, what's the weirdest job you've had?

378
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:36,670
What's the craziest or the best job or any experiences?

379
00:16:37,130 --> 00:16:37,250
Yes.

380
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:39,880
So visit our website, EnglishPod.com.

381
00:16:39,880 --> 00:16:41,650
Leave your questions and comments there on the website.

382
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:44,550
And we'll definitely be there to answer them.

383
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:45,770
And we hope to see you there.

384
00:16:46,080 --> 00:16:47,080
Thanks for listening and goodbye.

385
00:16:47,080 --> 00:16:52,080
Bye.
